Task 1: Creating Physical Volumes and Volume Groups
Subtask 1.1: Identify Available Disks
Before creating PVs, identify available disks:

lsblk
Expected Output:
Displays block devices (e.g., /dev/sdb, /dev/sdc).

Subtask 1.2: Create Physical Volumes
Use pvcreate to initialize disks as PVs:

sudo pvcreate /dev/sdb /dev/sdc
Verify with:

sudo pvs
Expected Output:
Lists PVs with details (e.g., /dev/sdb, /dev/sdc).

Subtask 1.3: Create a Volume Group
Combine PVs into a VG named my_vg:

sudo vgcreate my_vg /dev/sdb /dev/sdc
Verify with:

sudo vgs
Expected Output:
Shows my_vg with allocated PVs.

Task 2: Allocating and Resizing Logical Volumes
Subtask 2.1: Create a Logical Volume
Allocate a 5GB LV named my_lv:

sudo lvcreate -L 5G -n my_lv my_vg
Verify with:

sudo lvs
Expected Output:
Displays my_lv with 5GB size.

Subtask 2.2: Format and Mount the LV
Format as ext4 and mount:

sudo mkfs.ext4 /dev/my_vg/my_lv
sudo mkdir /mnt/mylv
sudo mount /dev/my_vg/my_lv /mnt/mylv
Verify:

df -h /mnt/mylv
Expected Output:
Shows mounted LV with 5GB capacity.

Subtask 2.3: Extend the Logical Volume
Extend my_lv by 2GB:

sudo lvextend -L +2G /dev/my_vg/my_lv
sudo resize2fs /dev/my_vg/my_lv
Verify:

df -h /mnt/mylv
Expected Output:
Shows increased size (~7GB).

Task 3: Creating and Managing Snapshots
Subtask 3.1: Create a Snapshot
Create a 1GB snapshot of my_lv:

sudo lvcreate -L 1G -s -n my_snapshot /dev/my_vg/my_lv
Verify:

sudo lvs
Expected Output:
Shows my_snapshot under my_vg.

Subtask 3.2: Restore from Snapshot
Unmount LV, restore, and remount:

sudo umount /mnt/mylv
sudo lvconvert --merge /dev/my_vg/my_snapshot
sudo mount /dev/my_vg/my_lv /mnt/mylv
Expected Output:
Reverts LV to the state when the snapshot was taken.

